Ladybird keeps a scrollbar thumb with automatic colors visible over
content of either lightness by outlining it in the opposite color to its
own. Gecko’s macOS painter does the same: The ThumbRect returned by
ScrollbarDrawingCocoa’s GetThumbRect() gives a white thumb a translucent
black stroke color, and a black thumb a translucent white one. WebKit’s
AdwaitaScrollbarPainter::paint() rings an overlay thumb with a 1px
border in the opposite color, from overlayThumbBorderColorDark and
overlayThumbBorderColorLight. And Chromium’s NativeThemeAura::
PaintScrollbarThumb() strokes an overlay thumb in a contrasting color.
None of them looks at the content under the thumb: Blink’s
ScrollableArea::RecalculateOverlayScrollbarColorScheme(), WebKit’s
LocalFrameView::recalculateScrollbarOverlayStyle(), and Gecko’s
nsNativeTheme::IsDarkBackgroundForScrollbar() all choose a light or a
dark thumb from a background color, much as Ladybird’s
scrollbar_colors_for_paint() does.
